course: dictionary
lesson: 10490
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. A dictionary gives each meaning a number, so you can choose the one that fits. Turnip is a good example: it is a vegetable, and it is also an old, informal word for a stupid person.
explain: The words around a word show you which meaning is right. «The bedroom is half bedchamber and half loft, half full of Badger's winter stores: apples, turnips, potatoes, nuts and jars of honey.» Turnips sit in a list with apples and potatoes, and they are stored for winter, so they must be food.
explain: Test each meaning by putting it into the sentence. Could a stupid person be stored with the apples and the honey? No, so the vegetable meaning is the right one. Always check every meaning before you decide.
fact: A turnip is a round root vegetable with white flesh and a white and purple skin, and it stores food for the plant.
fact: «Turnip Townshend» grew turnips to feed cattle in winter.
fact: In Scotland and the north of England, «turnip» or «neep» may mean the bigger yellow swede.
fact: Calling someone a turnip is an old, informal insult meaning they are stupid. It is not kind, so do not use it.
fact: In Ireland and Scotland, people carved turnips as Halloween lanterns before pumpkins were used.
exercise: «The bedroom is half bedchamber and half loft, half full of Badger's winter stores: apples, turnips, potatoes, nuts and jars of honey.» What are the turnips in this sentence? | *A vegetable stored for winter | Stupid people | Small rooms in a loft | Lanterns for Halloween
exercise: The dictionary gives turnip a second meaning, marked as old and informal. What is that meaning? | A Halloween lantern | *Someone who is stupid | A round root vegetable | A yellow swede in Scotland
exercise: A Scottish child says «neep» for a big yellow root. What might neep mean? | A pumpkin | A grey, wet day | A potato | *A bigger yellow swede
test: Which farmer's name is linked to growing turnips to feed cattle in winter? | Turnip Badger | Apple Jones | Pumpkin Parker | *Turnip Townshend
test: Which is the best way to check a word's meaning in a sentence? | Look only at the first word | Guess and move on without looking | *Put the meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Ignore the sentence
exam: Which statement about turnips is true? | Turnips are only eaten at Halloween | Turnips grow on tall trees | *A turnip is a round root vegetable with white flesh and white and purple skin | Turnips are always blue
